    {
      "id": "sailmaker",
      "flavor": "Sails turned cloth into a machine for catching wind, and the idea is old: Egyptian and Mesopotamian boats carried linen sails, while Pacific and Asian traditions made mats and cloth of their own. Early sails were woven near the shore, so weaver and sailmaker were often one person.\n\nSailmakers cut panels, sewed strong seams, hemmed the edges and treated the cloth with oil or tar against water and rot. They worked to the hull's shape and the wind's strength. The craft followed trade routes and shipyards, and its patterns changed as rigs grew complex.",
      "name": "Sailmaker",
      "icon": "profession_sailmaker.png",
      "description": "Weaves, cuts and treats heavy sailcloth for boats and mills."
