    {
      "id": "boatbuilder",
      "flavor": "Boats were built independently wherever waterways mattered: dugouts, reed bundles, skin frames and planked hulls appear in Africa, the Americas, Asia and the Pacific, often without contact. The problem was always the same — a frame light enough to float and stiff enough to carry.\n\nBuilders shaped timber, lashed or pegged the frame, added planks and sealed seams with tar, resin or clay; some traditions used no metal. Designs reflected local waters: shallow rivers, open coasts or islands. The craft travelled with trade, and each region kept its own way of joining a hull.",
      "name": "Boatbuilder",
      "icon": "profession_boatbuilder.png",
