    {
      "id": "butcher",
      "flavor": "Butchery is as old as domestication: once animals were kept, killing and dividing the carcass followed. Stone and bone knives preceded metal, and customs differed — halal and kosher rules, pig butchery in East Asia, llama and camel butchery in the Andes and Central Asia.\n\nButchers bled and dressed the animal, split the hide, removed organs and cut meat into pieces for cooking, drying or salting. Nothing was wasted: fat became tallow, skin became leather, bones and sinew served other trades. The work was often done outdoors, shaped by custom, season and household need.",
      "name": "Butcher",
      "icon": "slaughterhouse.png",
      "description": "Cuts livestock into meat, hides and tallow for the city."
